      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly avows love for Lear, presenting her loving state as true. The surrounding qualification limits its extent but does not weaken the expression's clear truth-conditional avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"since, I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The expression is immediately governed by “According to my bond,” so this occurrence affirms Cordelia's existing filial bond and positions her love as part of the duties owed under it. Her next turn specifies those duties as obedience, love, and honour. This invokes and renews allegiance to an existing parent-child obligation rather than creating a new promise or an unlimited commitment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The utterance is a controlled, deliberately bounded answer rather than a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly measures the declaration by her bond and refuses excess; her difficulty verbalising feeling does not itself turn the words into an involuntary outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth:\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a principled, limiting answer to Lear's competitive demand that his daughters say who loves him most. Cordelia rejects hyperbolic or exclusive profession by defining her love as exactly proportionate to her bond and later explaining that marriage would claim part of her love, care, and duty.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Half my love with him, half my care and duty:\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks each daughter to declare how much she loves him, with a more opulent share offered in response. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives the focal avowal as a restrained alternative to her sisters' expansive professions, expressly tying it to her filial bond.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms an existing filial undertaking: she is bound to return the fitting duties of a daughter, including obedience, love, and honour. The terms are deliberately limited—“no more nor less”—and she denies that filial love should exclude the love and duties she may owe a future husband. Lear does not accept this formulation as an adequate response; he tells her to “Mend your speech a little,” so the affirmation receives adverse rather than confirming uptake. Later supplied passages provide additional evidence of enduring affection and care, but they are not separate promises that create the original utterance's undertaking force.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"This is William Shakespeare’s King Lear, Act I, scene 1, commonly known as the love-test scene.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied passages strongly support the avowal, invocation of filial duty, and evidence of genuine affection. They do not fully specify every practical obligation contained in Cordelia's “bond,” nor do they show Lear accepting her bounded formulation at this moment.\"}"
        }
      ],
